{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nThe root tree holds 6,008 flat ticket directories and is 270 KB; every commit re-emits the WHOLE\nroot tree, so tree bytes are ~95% of the blobless pack. Sharding the flat namespace shrinks the\nper-commit root-tree object so each re-emission is far smaller — a durable structural reduction of\nthe tree churn that dominates the pack, complementing S1 (historical) and W1 (write-rate).\n\n## Scope\n\nShard the flat `tickets/<id>` layout into a fan-out namespace using the first two hex characters\nof the canonical ticket id: `tickets/<first-two-hex>/<id>`. A single-ticket mutation should then\nre-emit the changed ticket tree, its small shard tree, and a bounded top-level fan-out tree rather\nthan one flat top-level tree containing every ticket. Preserve ticket resolution\n(alias/short-id/full-id), event replay, and reconcile/merge-as-union behavior.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nThe history reclaim (S1), write-batching (W1), and the live shared-store cutover. This is the\non-disk tree LAYOUT only. The live `.tickets-tracker` migration/cutover sequencing is an operator\ndecision and will not be performed by this task.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Ticket storage writes new events under `tickets/<first-two-hex>/<id>` and a fixture mutation\n      proves the top-level tree bytes are at least 80% smaller than an equivalent flat layout;\n      verified by `tests/unit/test_store_sharded_layout.py`.\n- [ ] Ticket resolution (alias, short-id, full-id) and event replay are unchanged in mixed and\n      migrated stores; verified by `tests/unit/test_store_sharded_layout_heldout.py` and existing\n      resolver/replay selectors.\n- [ ] The ensure-registry migration maps every existing flat ticket directory to its deterministic\n      shard without loss, reassignment, or leftover flat ticket dirs, and a second run is a no-op;\n      verified by `tests/unit/test_store_sharded_layout.py`.\n- [ ] The migration's collision guard is defined and tested: two distinct source paths mapping to\n      the same target abort the ensure run as a fatal/permanent operator-repair error before any\n      partial move is committed; verified by `tests/unit/test_store_sharded_layout.py`.\n- [ ] Reconcile/merge-as-union behavior is preserved on a two-clone reconvergence fixture with\n      sharded ticket writes; both sides' commits remain reachable after reconvergence, verified by\n      `tests/unit/test_store_sharded_layout_heldout.py`.\n\n## Implementation Plan\n\n1. Add `rebar._store.ticket_layout` as the single owner for ticket layout. It will provide:\n   `ticket_shard(id)`, `ticket_relpath(id)`, `ticket_dir(tracker, id)`, `existing_ticket_dir`,\n   `iter_ticket_dirs`, `iter_ticket_ids`, and `migrate_flat_ticket_dirs_unit`. The resolver keeps\n   returning separator-free canonical ids/names; the helper, not resolver return values, composes\n   the two-segment on-disk path.\n2. Update writers to publish through the helper. `_store/staging.py` computes the sharded target\n   and git-relative path; lifecycle transaction writers in `_commands/txn.py`, `claim.py`, and\n   `transition.py` use the same helper for under-lock state checks and event path composition.\n3. Update all flat id readers/walkers that currently compose `tracker/<id>` or scan the tracker\n   root as ticket ids. The explicit consumer inventory is: `_ids.py`; `reducer/_api.py` and\n   `reducer/_inbound.py`; `_engine_support/{field_reads,lookups,reads,validate,next_batch}.py`;\n   `attest/authorship.py`; `signing.py`; `_store/gitutil.py`; `_snapshot/{ticket_view,ticket_objects}.py`; metrics `{blame,event_metrics,sidecar_metrics,bug_trends}`; graph\n   modules `{_cache,_graph,_hierarchy,_links,_status}`; commands `{claim,transition,txn,delete,\n   compact,compact_recovery,compact_trigger,close_precheck,close_disposition,fsck_scan,\n   fsck_repair,fsck_tracker_health,identity,session_log,tracker_maintenance,verify_authorship}`;\n   LLM sidecar/readers `{completion_sidecar,code_review/sidecar,overlap/queue,enrich_drain,\n   plan_review/claimability,plan_review/relation_snapshot,plan_review/sidecar,epic_bug_screen,\n   workflow/recorder}`; `_io/{export_ndjson,import_ndjson}.py`; `_engine_support/commit_impact.py`; and reconciler local readers\n   `{transition_replay,local_label_intent,impossible_links}.py`.\n4. Update resolver semantics without changing its public contract. `_scan_tracker_root`,\n   `_dir_prefix_matches`, `build_resolver_scan_index`, and the exact-dir fast path must build the\n   same sorted id/name index from both flat legacy dirs and sharded dirs; aliases, ambiguous\n   prefixes, deprecated one-quad behavior, and full-id resolution stay byte-identical.\n5. Add an idempotent School-B ensure unit (`ticket-layout-shards`) registered in `_store.ensures`.\n   It runs under the existing ensure sweep's write lock, precomputes the full move plan before\n   mutating, refuses collisions atomically, moves only flat ticket directories, removes now-empty\n   shard parents only on rollback, stages old+new paths, and commits one migration commit only when\n   drift exists. It registers `sharded-ticket-layout` in `compat.KNOWN_CAPABILITIES` as the expand half,\n   then appends that store-compat capability in the same migration path so older v1.0+\n   binaries fail closed while the migrating/current binary continues to read the store.\n6. Keep compatibility during the expand phase: before migration, new code reads both layouts and\n   writes an existing flat ticket in place until the migration has moved it; after migration, all\n   writes land in shards. No live shared-store migration/cutover is executed by this task.\n\n7. Make fsck/store-health depth-aware: shard directories are recognized as layout containers, not\n   foreign ticket data. A top-level non-dot directory is ticket data only when it directly holds\n   event files OR is a valid shard containing ticket directories; foreign-store-path diagnostics\n   still fire for unrelated top-level directories.\n8. Update pinned snapshot tree readers (`_snapshot/ticket_objects.py`) to parse both flat\n   `<id>/<event>` and sharded `<shard>/<id>/<event>` paths through the layout helper, preserving\n   resolver/id extraction for attested ticket views.\n\n## Surface-Parity Census\n\n- CLI command: in scope, proven through subprocess `rebar create/comment/show` style fixture tests.\n- MCP/API tool: out of scope for live MCP execution because this task changes the shared code and the\n  MCP server may be running older code; parity is accepted because MCP write tools delegate to the\n  same library/core paths covered here.\n- Library facade (`import rebar`): in scope, proven by library create/show/reduce tests on fixtures.\n- Automated callers (reconciler, workflows, bots, LLM sidecars): in scope for path routing by updating\n  the inventory above and running targeted unit selectors; no live Jira/LLM calls are needed.\n- Dual-direction: in scope for local read/write and import/export; outbound Jira field semantics are\n  unchanged because this is only ticket-store layout.\n\n## Testing / Verification\n\nTargeted RED/GREEN proof will run:\n\n- `python -m pytest tests/unit/test_store_sharded_layout.py tests/unit/test_store_sharded_layout_heldout.py -q`\n- Existing compatibility selectors: resolver/replay tests under `tests/scripts/reducer/`, graph/store\n  tests touching ticket paths, and reconvergence tests.\n- Pre-flight gate: `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and `make test` with the worktree `.venv/bin` first\n  on `PATH`.\n\n## Deployment / Rollback\n\nThis code is the expand/read+write+migration mechanism, not the live cutover. The migration is\nidempotent and fixture-tested, but applying it to the shared live store remains an operator decision.\nOnce an operator runs the ensure/migration on a store, the compat capability makes older v1.0+\nbinaries fail closed; rollback is restoring/reverting the migrated tickets-branch commit or using an\noperator-approved store-maintenance path, not an agent force/bypass.\n\n## Portability\n\nPure store-layout change with an in-process, idempotent ensure-registry migration; no dependence on\nany single CI provider. Migration validation is confined to fixture/throwaway stores; no live-store\ncutover is performed."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788239794160604001,"uuid":"a01644b2-e646-45f1-ac0d-c8293b68aa97"}